# Glossary

Each entry says what the thing is, then how Resonance uses it.

**netns.** Short for network namespace. A separate network stack with
its own interfaces, addresses, and routes. Resonance puts each node
in its own netns so packets must pass through the simulated radio.

**TUN.** A virtual network card. Programs read and write packets
through a file descriptor instead of a wire. Resonance opens one TUN
per node and pumps packets between them.

**gossip.** Nodes telling each other what they know, in rounds.
Concord uses gossip for membership plus workload state; weld uses
DDS discovery instead. Gossip is one SUT pattern, not the harness.

**memberlist.** The gossip library Concord uses for membership. It
probes peers, marks suspects down, and agrees on who is alive.

**cgroup.** Short for control group. A kernel grouping that limits
and tracks processes. Resonance puts each node in its own cgroup
subtree (Concord nodes get per-node plus daemon subtrees).

**Noise.** A framework for encrypted channels. Concord wraps peer
traffic in Noise sessions keyed per node.

**epoch.** A generation number. Each reassignment of work bumps the
epoch so survivors can tell new assignments from stale ones.

**tombstone.** A marker that says deleted. A stopped workload leaves a
tombstone so reunion does not revive it.

**journal.** An append-only log of what happened. Concord keeps one
per node and scenarios grep it; weld echo nodes hold no state and
keep none. Journal is a Concord SUT shape, not a harness shape.

**kv store.** Short for key-value store. A small database of keys
to values. Concord keeps workload state there beside the journal;
weld keeps none.

**registry.** An OCI server that stores container images. Each SUT
node that serves images runs its own registry on port 8444 in
image tests. Concord image tests run one per node; localhost
registries are the test pattern.

**catalog.** The list of images a registry holds. Tests read it back
after pushing to prove the push landed.

**allocator.** The run-id dispenser. `sim_run_acquire` hands out ids
1..154 through a file lock so parallel runs map to disjoint names.

**isolate_all.** The `SimNet` flag that puts every node in its own
netns. Set means no host footprint. Clear means node 0 stays in the
host namespace, which is the frozen check/ default.

**underlay.** The harness's own wiring. Underlay addresses
(`192.168.100.0/24`) connect TUN devices; the harness moves those
packets and applies radio physics to them.

**overlay.** The address range the binaries use among themselves
(`10.0.0.0/16`). Binaries form their own mesh there and never see
the underlay.

**SUT.** Short for system under test. The binary the harness measures:
Concord or Weld.

**hold.** A fixed wait after a signal. The `until` condition latches,
then the phase waits out the full `hold_s` before advancing.

**phase.** One step of a scenario. A phase runs its `do` steps on
entry, then waits for `until` or holds `sustain` until `timeout_s`.

**sustain.** A condition that must hold for the whole phase timeout.
Any violation fails at once. Used for holds that must never break,
such as a tombstone staying absent.

**forensics.** What a failed run saves before cleanup. Log tails,
journal copies, and config dumps, collected while the files still
exist and before the statedir wipe.

**ROS.** Short for Robot Operating System. A framework for robot
software. Weld tests use ROS peers beside echo nodes for pub/sub
round trips.

**DDS.** Short for Data Distribution Service. The messaging layer ROS
uses to find peers. Weld discovery traffic passes through the mesh
like any other packet.

**mDNS.** Short for multicast DNS. Shouting on the local network to
find peers. Blinding multicast turns this channel off while unicast
keeps flowing.

**frac.** Short for fraction. A progress line the executor prints as
`suite: frac <phase> <num> <den>`. Sources are real events only:
done `do` steps, `until` node-set changes, and once per second on
`hold_s` or `sustain` dwells.

**needs.** The list of abilities a YAML file requires. Unknown `needs`
verbs fail lint; at run time unknown verbs skip. Each entry names a
driver ability such as `workload.submit` or `weld.pub`.

**do.** The list of steps a phase runs on entry. Steps name faults
and sends: `drop`, `heal`, `move`, `kill`, `restart`, `submit`,
`stop`, `pub`.

**until.** The condition a phase waits for, then holds. The phase
polls until the condition turns true, latches, waits out the full
`hold_s`, and then advances. Flap clears the latch.

**hold_s.** The fixed wait after an `until` condition latches. It
needs `until` and must not exceed the phase `timeout_s`.

**timeout_s.** The backstop in seconds. A phase fails when it runs
past its own `timeout_s`; a file fails when it runs past the
file-level `timeout_s`.

**observed.** A weld-only round trip check. It asks whether the text
sent from one node shows up where it should. Resonance checks the
weld log, plus the ROS capture where a peer runs beside that node.

**mesh_count.** A liveness count with no `on` key. It reports how
many nodes look alive right now. On concord it reads the CLI alive
count; on weld it counts live children.

**ros_peers.** The list of ROS sidecars a weld file starts. Each
entry names a node plus a distro (`humble`, `jazzy`, or
`rolling`). Omit it when no `observed` check needs a ROS log.

**durability.** What a `restart` keeps. Five names: `fresh`
(wiped home, stranger), `preserve` (same home), `rekey_rotate`
(same home, bumped key), `key_wipe` (same home, fresh key,
same generation), `wipe_state` (same id and key, empty
journal plus store). Weld allows `fresh` and `preserve` only.

**skew.** A concord spawn variant that shifts one node clock by
the `offset` string. Used to test time behavior.

**anchor.** A concord spawn variant that starts one node as the
provisioned rendezvous and points configs at it. Used to test
discovery without multicast.

**UDP-vs-TUN.** Two packet paths. One path carries simulated packets
and the other carries real packets. UDP (`udp/udp.h`) is in-process
delivery for tests with no binaries. TUN (`tun/tun.h`) is real
packets through real devices for binaries. SUT binaries never call
UDP; they use kernel sockets and a TUN (Concord binaries are the
example). Use UDP for `make
check` and small experiments; use TUN when a real binary must talk.

**exit code.** How a child or helper reports done. Zero means ok,
nonzero means fail, 127 means the child could not start. `weld_pub`
needs exit 0 plus the sent confirmation; `run_teardown` children
exit after TERM then KILL; suite files map helper results to pass,
fail, or skip.

**ProgView.** One progress row (`cmd/progress.h`). It records which
file, which phase, and how far. It holds the base name, the last
phase text, a live flag, plus a 0-based phase index and total for
known files. Zero total means unknown. `prog_want` gates drawing on
tty without tap or json; `prog_color` gates color on TERM plus
NO_COLOR.

**run verdict.** What setup reports (`run/run.h`). Values are go,
skip, or fail. `RUN_OK` means go, `RUN_SKIP` means a precondition is
missing such as TUN permission, `RUN_FAIL` means a hard error. TUN
open failure is a skip; the rest is a fail.

**suite verdict.** What one file reports (`suite/exec.h`). Values
are pass, fail, or skip with a reason. `SUITE_PASS` means the file
passed, `SUITE_FAIL` means it failed, `SUITE_SKIP` means its needs
or binaries are absent.

**why buffer.** Where a suite call writes its reason. It is a text
slot for skip or failure. Callers pass `why` plus `why_n`; the
callee writes at most `why_n` bytes with NUL end, empty on success,
skip reason or first failure text on skip or fail.

**TUN map.** Which device belongs to which node (`tun/tun.h`). It is
a table from file descriptor and IP to node id. `TunMap` holds up to
`TUN_MAP_MAX` slots; `tun_map_add` records one; `tun_pump_fd` reads
from one slot and forwards through the radio check.

**SimNet.** One run network names (`sim/sim.h`). It holds the seven
names that keep parallel runs apart. Statedir, netns prefix, TUN
prefix and base, underlay /24, cgroup root, plus `isolate_all`. Run
0 is the historic default; `sim_run_acquire` plus
`sim_net_try_for_run` mint disjoint values for runs 1..154.

**agree.** Nodes reporting the same assignment. In Concord journals
one `AssignedNodeID` per node is recorded; unanimity means all
nodes agree. Resonance `agree` holds when each node reports the same
non-nil id for every member of the named set.

**assigned_on.** Placement on one node. In Concord journals the
node each workload was assigned to is recorded. Resonance
`assigned_on` holds when the named node reports a non-nil
`AssignedNodeID` for every member of the named set.

**image.** The container a workload runs. In Concord journals it
is recorded per node. Resonance `image` holds when every `on`
node journal reports the wanted value for every member of the
named set, and `update` moves the set to a new value.

**bins.** The per-node binary map. A top-level table from node
index to binary path for mixed-version runs. Resonance boots the
default binary, then preserve-restarts each listed concord node
into its binary once; weld spawns each node straight into its
bin. Missing binaries skip the run.

**nomcast.** A phase with multicast blinded. `multicast: off`
pumps that phase through `tun_pump_fd_nomcast`, so IPv4 multicast
goes blind while unicast keeps flowing. Resonance uses it with an
anchor spawn to prove discovery without multicast.

**qos.** Shaping on one pump path (`tun/tun.h`). Loss share, base
plus jitter delay, cap rate, dup chance, slow gate, seed. A `drop`
mapping arms it per fd; `heal` clears every slot at once.

**slow.** A deferred drain. `slow` arms `TunQos` `slow_secs` on the
listed nodes. Resonance uses it to prove the mesh survives a
stalled pump, then heals.

**replay.** A weld-only text log publish. One payload per line, in
order, from one node. Empty lines are skipped; lines cap at 1024
chars. Resonance uses it to replay captured chatter through pub.

**diskfill.** Concord disk pressure. `diskfill` fallocates the node
filler file to `mb` MB; 0 removes it. Resonance uses it to prove
behavior under a full state dir.

**diskreadonly.** Concord writability flip. `diskreadonly` runs
chmod a-w (on) or u+w (off) over the node state tree. It is an
approximation, not block-device RO. Resonance uses it to prove
read-only behavior, then reverses it.

**journalcorrupt.** Concord journal damage. It truncates the node
`journal.jsonl` then writes 8 bytes (`CORRUPT` plus newline), so
readers see invalid JSON. Resonance uses it to prove recovery
from a corrupt journal.

**rate.** Pacing between issues. `rate` on `submit` (and parsed on
`pub`) names msgs per sec. Resonance sleeps between counted
submits; single issues ignore it. Timing stays coarse at the 2 s
query rhythm.

**metric.** A measured number the run emits. Printed as a `metric`
timeline event with `key=value` text. Resonance emits `phase.<i>.ms`
and `maxrss.mb` each phase; `present.<name>.ms` emits when first
seen (first sight minus submit; same-ms reads 0 and is skipped).

**event.** One timeline line the run prints. Shaped as `suite: ev`
with time, kind, phase, message, and two numbers. Kinds are phase,
step, marker, metric, verdict, or cond. Resonance prints them for
phase begins and ends, applied steps, first marker sightings,
condition outcomes, metrics, and verdicts.

**disk cap.** A boot state size limit.
`disk_cap_mb` names MB of private tmpfs over
one Concord node state dir, 0 uncapped.
Resonance boots capped nodes through
`concord_spawn_ex`; restarts never carry a cap.

**benchmark.** Sustained load by reusing submit or pub in a
paced loop. Resonance runs one benchmark step per phase and
emits count, loss, p50, p99, max, target, overrun, plus
per-message latency and bucket counts.

**latency.** Per-message delay in milliseconds. Resonance
measures submit to list sighting or pub to observed log match;
sub-millisecond reads as 0. Report plots .lat series over time.

**histogram.** Bucketed latency counts. Resonance emits
.hist.<b> per bucket; the report draws one bar chart per stem
and phase with bars labeled by bucket index.

**overrun.** Shortfall flag. Resonance sets overrun to 1 when
issued fell short of wanted, else 0. It never fails the step;
the phase condition still gates pass.

**ramp.** An adaptive ceiling ladder. One benchmark step runs
rising rates itself, stopping on loss or p99 plus hard rate,
step, and time backstops. Resonance reads `ramp:` inside
`benchmark:` and emits per-step leaves plus a numeric ceiling
rate with a marker reason.

**ceiling.** The top passing rate of an adaptive ramp. Resonance
emits it as `bench.<ph>.<k>.ceiling.rate`, with the stop reason
(`loss`, `p99`, `max_rate`, `max_steps`, or `max_duration`) on a
marker event, never as text.

**warmup.** Leading issues sent but left out of stats and loss.
Resonance drops them before `count`, `loss`, and latency math;
`warmup` must sit below `count` when `count` is present.

**pong.** The echo reply channel. A benchmark relay republishes
each line byte identical to `<topic>/pong`; roundtrip latency is
reply sighting minus send.

**send_ns.** The sender timestamp in a bench payload. Monotonic ns
taken per issue at send; the observer subtracts it from the log
sighting to get latency.

**primitive.** One named send or observe verb in the probe
registry. Sends are `weld-pub`, `ros-pub`, `workload-submit`;
observes are `log-contains`, `ros-log-contains`, `journal-has`,
`workload-running`. Each wires to the existing driver path
(`suite/exec.h`).

**desugar.** The old sugar to registry mapping. Old `from`,
`on`, `topic`, `text`, `node`, `image`, `as`, and `flags`
keys mean the same send or expect entry. New `send` plus
`expect` blocks name the entries directly.

**placeholder.** A template slot in `payload` or `match`
text. Spelled `{{seq}}`, `{{send_ns}}`, `{{body}}`,
`{{wid}}`. Resonance expands them per issue; unknown
names fail lint and `wid` outside submit fails loudly.

**match rule.** How an `expect` proves an issue landed.
Log expects grep the full payload prefix, never content;
journal expects compare one field to one value; list
expects want the state `running`.
